Title: Market Architecture for Innovation Capital Formation: Coordination Conditions for Participation and Exchange in Innovation Asset Markets
Abstract: This paper examines the market architecture required for innovation assets to participate within coordinated capital allocation systems. Building on the economic foundations established in Research Paper No. 01, the analysis explores the institutional conditions under which heterogeneous actors—including researchers, corporations, venture investors, and institutional capital providers—can engage in innovation asset markets. The paper identifies the structural conditions required for coordinated market participation and the emergence of liquidity, including legibility across heterogeneous actors, participation density, structured expectation formation, staged risk transformation, temporal alignment between capital providers and innovation maturation, and governance synchronization across legal, financial, and institutional domains. The analysis further examines the operational mechanisms through which these conditions translate into functioning markets, including asset discoverability, signal formation, capital structuring across stages of innovation development, and market synchronization with broader capital market infrastructures. Together, these mechanisms describe how innovation assets—operationally expressed through intellectual property—can transition from isolated transactions toward repeatable market participation within capital allocation systems.
